{
  "schema": "efh.function-note/v1",
  "note_id": "FN-BITLSHIFT-001",
  "functions": [
    "FUNC.BITLSHIFT",
    "FUNC.BITRSHIFT"
  ],
  "kind": "documentation-differs",
  "title": "BITLSHIFT and BITRSHIFT return 0 for |shift_amount| in 49..=53 and bypass the shift-count bound when number is 0",
  "statement": "In Excel 16.0 build 20430, BITLSHIFT and BITRSHIFT deviate from Microsoft's published 48-bit result and 53-bit shift limits in two ways: (1) when number is 0, any finite shift_amount returns 0 (even BITLSHIFT(0, 54) = 0); (2) when number is nonzero and the truncated |shift_amount| is 49..=53, both functions return 0 instead of #NUM! (e.g. BITLSHIFT(1, 49) = 0 and BITLSHIFT(1, 53) = 0, whereas BITLSHIFT(1, 48) = #NUM! and BITLSHIFT(1, 54) = #NUM!).",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "Measured across Excel 16.0 build 20430 W111 bitwise discriminator rows in CalcFunc-Evidence cache/w111-bitwise-20260929/ and matched on 8,131/8,131 BITLSHIFT and 8,128/8,128 BITRSHIFT full-bank rows.",
  "inferred": null,
  "documentation": {
    "claim": "Microsoft's BITLSHIFT and BITRSHIFT pages state that if the result exceeds 2^48 - 1 (48 bits) or |shift_amount| > 53, the functions return #NUM!, without noting that zero operands ignore finite shift_amount > 53 or that |shift_amount| in 49..=53 returns 0 instead of #NUM!.",
    "source": "https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/bitlshift-function-c55bb27e-cacd-4c7c-b258-d80861a03c9c; https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/bitrshift-function-274d6996-f42c-4743-abdb-4ff95351222c"
  },
